   "text": {
    "L1": "Water can change state without disappearing. Two important changes are evaporation and condensation.",
    "L2": "Evaporation is liquid water changing into water vapour from the surface.",
    "L3": "It happens slowly at all temperatures, so clothes and wet floors can dry without boiling.",
    "L4": "Water vapour is in the air. When it cools on a cold glass or mirror, it changes into tiny liquid drops.",
    "L5": "Those drops come from the surrounding air, not through the glass.",
    "L6": "Together, evaporation and condensation drive the water cycle: sea water becomes vapour, clouds form, and rain falls.",
    "L7": "So remember: evaporation changes liquid to vapour, while condensation changes vapour to liquid.",
    "L8": "Now identify the process that forms drops on the outside of a cold bottle."
   },
